ject with history and retained_indices. So we need to handle gracefully. Let's look at fixtures: \n- case 000: forced=[4], round_targets [6,4]. N=8. forced index 4. In first round target 6, we need to select 6 out of 8, include forced 4. In second round, from the selected 6, we need to select 4, including forced 4. That's feasible.\n- case 001: forced=[2], targets [6,4] feasible.\n- case 002: forced=[3], targets [6,4] feasible.\nSo all feasible. We can assume feasibility.\n\nAlgorithm: For each round, we have list of active indices. We have scores for each original index. We want to select top k (k=round_targets[i]) by score among active, but we must include forced indices regardless of score. Also preserve original order. So we can:\n- Compute set of forced indices that are in active.\n- Determine how many additional slots: k - len(forced_set_in_active).\n- Among active indices that are not forced, select the top additional_slots by score (descending). Then combine forced and selected non-forced, then sort by original order to preserve order. That gives the new active list.\n\nWe need to output history: a list of lists of integers? The schema says history is an array of arrays of integers. So each round's mask? It says \"per-round masks\". So history should contain the list of indices selected at each round? Or the list of active indices after each round? Typically \"history\" would be the sequence of retained index sets. But the schema says \"history\": array of arrays of integers. It doesn't specify length. It likely contains the mask (list of indices) for each round. And \"retained_indices\" is the final list after all rounds.\n\nThe output contract: return {\"history\": [...], \"retained_indices\": [...]}. So history is list of lists, each list being the indices retained after that round (or maybe the indices selected in that round? Need to infer from description: \"Return the per-round masks and the final retained indices.\" So history is the per-round masks, likely the set of indices after each round (including initial full set?). But the initial full set is not a round. The rounds are the pruning rounds. So after first round we have first mask, after second round second mask, etc. And final retained_indices is same as last mask? Possibly. Or maybe retained_indices is same as history[-1]? But the contract requires both keys. It could be redundant but we'll output both.\n\nWe'll compute:\nactive = list(range(N))\nhistory = []\nfor i in range(num_rounds):\n    scores = round_scores[i]\n    target = round_targets[i]\n    forced_set = set(forced) & set(active)\n    num_forced = len(forced_set)\n    if num_forced > target:\n        # handle infeasibility - maybe just keep forced and ignore target? But per contract reject. We'll assume not happening.\n        # For robustness, set target = num_forced and proceed? But then we might have fewer than target. Alternative: we could skip? I think it's safe to assume feasible.\n        pass\n    # Get non-forced active indices\n    non_forced = [idx for idx in active if idx not in forced_set]\n    # Sort non_forced by score descending (taking score from scores list at that index)\n    non_forced_sorted = sorted(non_forced, key=lambda idx: scores[idx], reverse=True)\n    # Take top (target - num_forced) from non_forced_sorted\n    selected_non_forced = non_forced_sorted[:target - num_forced]\n    # Combine forced and selected non-forced\n    new_active = list(forced_set) + selected_non_forced\n    # Preserve original order: sort new_active by original index (ascending)\n    new_active.sort()\n    active = new_active\n    history.append(active.copy())\nretained_indices = active\nreturn {\"history\": history, \"retained_indices\": retained_indices}\n\nBut careful: The forced indices should be included in each round? The description says \"Insert forced indices before filling remaining positions.\" That suggests that in each round, we first include all forced indices that are still activ